{
  "id": "no5mant",
  "title": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
  "order": 56,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ful235z",
      "content": "Electricity and magnetism are closely related branches of physics. Electricity deals with the flow of electric charges, while magnetism deals with the magnetic properties of materials and magnetic fields produced by electric currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Core Topics",
          "value": "10 Sections",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Governing Law",
          "value": "Ohm's Law (V=IR)",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit: Current",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit: Resistance",
          "value": "Ohm (Ω)",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "itgukry"
    },
    {
      "id": "65je7wd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is the rate of flow of electric charge, measured in Amperes (A).",
        "Ohm’s Law defines the fundamental linear relationship between Voltage, Current, and Resistance (V = IR).",
        "Resistance depends on the conductor material, length, cross-sectional area, and temperature.",
        "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is transferred or consumed, expressed as P = VI.",
        "Electromagnets utilize electric current flowing through a coil to generate a temporary magnetic field."
      ],
      "id": "bzku0s4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7we5s7d",
      "content": "Electricity is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. Electric current is the movement of electrons through a conductor such as copper wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gybp7of",
      "content": "• Electric charge is a fundamental property of matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a4edux7",
      "content": "• Electric current is the flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3pz39hm",
      "content": "• The SI unit of electric current is Ampere (A)."
    },
    {
      "id": "1kuikae",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Electric Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ds25lcb",
      "content": "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "35mp6zz",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lmcnbob",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "u2crku4",
      "content": "I = Electric current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4rsce44",
      "content": "Q = Electric charge"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "762fzaq",
      "content": "t = Time"
    },
    {
      "id": "c3xcuyu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Voltage"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "60d6qa2",
      "content": "Voltage is the potential difference between two points in an electric circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fo9qfbh",
      "content": "SI Unit of Voltage = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "px2saxp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Resistance"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Electricity and Magnetism",
      "id": "idbzyc7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rx1zj4x",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered by a conductor to the flow of electric current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zrzqlgi",
      "content": "SI Unit of Resistance = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "id": "drpq9pp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x8v7dun",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0y19nq3",
      "content": "Formula: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "id": "f0jqmuu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Electric Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cyxrko3",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "mstxcjw",
      "content": "Formula: P = VI"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "sddt88z",
      "content": "SI Unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q8uww1t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Electric Circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Series Circuit",
        "Parallel Circuit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Current (I)",
          "Same current flows through all components",
          "Total current divides across parallel branches"
        ],
        [
          "Voltage (V)",
          "Total voltage is divided across components",
          "Same voltage exists across all parallel branches"
        ],
        [
          "Equivalent Resistance",
          "Increases: R_total = R1 + R2 + ...",
          "Decreases: 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + ..."
        ],
        [
          "Fault Impact",
          "Break in any point stops current in entire circuit",
          "Break in one branch does not affect other branches"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "o3zh98c"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc9fai0",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
